"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} (the distribution of galaxies in space: clusters) and §{#} (the challenge of dark matter). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> four views, chosen above the picture. Zwicky’s speeds: Zwicky’s puzzle: only the stars; Add the unseen mass; Stars and gas are not enough; The book’s cluster: {#} km/s. Hot gas: Gas at {#} million K; Hot gas without dark matter. Lensing: Arcs from a background galaxy; An Einstein ring; Lensing by the stars and gas alone. The Bullet Cluster: The Bullet Cluster today; The Bullet Cluster with no dark matter; Before the collision. This worksheet uses all four views.",
"The cluster is simulated: a ball of dark matter, hot gas and {#} galaxies, set by its total mass inside {#} kpc (<strong>Total mass inside {#} kpc</strong>; click its readout to type a value such as {#}). In the model {#}% of the mass is in stars, {#}% in hot gas and {#}% in dark matter.",
"<strong>What pulls</strong> lets all the mass pull, or only the stars and gas, or only the stars.",
"Zwicky’s speeds colors each galaxy by its speed toward us (blue) or away (red), the only part of its motion a redshift can give, and shows how widely those speeds spread, σ. The readouts turn the spread into the mass the speeds need.",
"Hot gas gives the gas temperature, where its glow peaks, and the mass needed to hold the gas in. Lensing traces the light of a galaxy far behind the cluster and gives the Einstein ring and the mass inside it. The Bullet Cluster is a simple model of two clusters that have just passed through each other, with the galaxies (yellow), the hot gas (pink) and the mass map that lensing would show (green lines).",
"Record reading stores, in the Zwicky’s speeds view, the true mass, what pulls, the time, the number of galaxies inside {#} kpc, σ, the orbit speed, the mass the speeds need and the mass in stars. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the first Data table below.",
"Weigh a cluster of galaxies three ways, from the speeds of its galaxies, from its hot gas and from the way it bends light, and find out how much of its mass we cannot see.",
"<strong>Zwicky’s puzzle.</strong> In {#} Fritz Zwicky measured how fast the galaxies in the Coma cluster move. A cluster holds together only if its gravity can keep its fastest galaxies from flying off, and Zwicky found the galaxies far too fast for the mass he could see. The same rule that weighs a planet’s orbit weighs the cluster: galaxies orbiting at speed v at radius R need a mass",
"\\[M = \\frac{v^2 R}{G}\\]",
"inside R. With R in kpc, v in km/s and M in solar masses, \\(G = 4.30\\times10^{-6}\\) kpc (km/s)²/M☉. A redshift gives only the part of a galaxy’s speed toward or away from us. In this cluster the spread of those speeds, σ, is related to the orbit speed by \\(v = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\sigma\\).",
"<strong>Hot gas.</strong> X-ray telescopes show that clusters are full of gas at about \\(10^8\\) K, with more mass in the gas than in all the stars of the galaxies. Gas that hot moves at over {#} km/s and would escape unless gravity held it in, so its temperature weighs the cluster too. By Wien’s law, a glow at temperature T peaks at the wavelength",
"\\[\\lambda_{\\text{peak}} = \\frac{2.90\\times10^6\\ \\text{nm K}}{T}.\\]",
"<strong>Bent light.</strong> Mass bends light. A galaxy right behind a cluster can be stretched into arcs, or into a whole ring, the Einstein ring. The ring’s size gives the mass inside it, whatever that mass is made of.",
"<strong>The Bullet Cluster.</strong> Two clusters crashed through each other, about {#} million years before the light we see left them. Their galaxies, far apart from one another, sailed through. Their hot gas clouds, most of the ordinary mass, slammed together and stalled in the middle, where X-rays show them. Lensing then shows where most of the mass is.",
"Galaxies in a cluster {#} kpc across orbit at about {#} km/s, so R = {#} kpc. Find the cluster’s mass in solar masses.",
"A cluster’s gas is at \\(1.0\\times10^8\\) K. Where does its glow peak, and in what part of the spectrum is that?",
"A cluster’s lensing gives \\(1.0\\times10^{15}\\) M☉. If {#}% of it is stars, {#}% hot gas and the rest dark matter, how much of each?",
"In the Bullet Cluster the hot gas, which holds most of the ordinary mass, is stuck in the middle, while the galaxies have moved on to either side. Predict where the lensing mass map will peak: on the gas in the middle, on the galaxies, or spread evenly between them. Give your reason before you open the demo.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Zwicky’s puzzle.</strong> The page opens with <strong>Zwicky’s puzzle: only the stars</strong>: only the stars, {#}% of the mass, pull. Press <strong>Play</strong> and watch the galaxies for a few seconds, then press <strong>Reset</strong>.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Weighing with speeds.</strong> Press <strong>Add the unseen mass</strong> (all the mass pulls, \\(1.00\\times10^{15}\\) M☉). Press <strong>Record reading</strong> before you press Play. Set <strong>Total mass inside {#} kpc</strong> to \\(2\\times10^{14}\\) (click its readout, type {#} and press Enter) and record. Do the same at \\(5\\times10^{14}\\), \\(1.5\\times10^{15}\\) and \\(2\\times10^{15}\\) M☉. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>, then fill the last column, σ², yourself.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Hot gas.</strong> Press <strong>Hot gas</strong> above the picture, then <strong>Gas at {#} million K</strong>. In the second Data table write the <strong>Gas temperature T</strong>, the <strong>Glow peaks at (Wien)</strong>, the <strong>Mass inside {#} kpc from the gas</strong>, the <strong>Mass of the gas itself</strong> and the <strong>Mass in the stars</strong>. Then press <strong>Hot gas without dark matter</strong> and <strong>Play</strong>, and watch what the gas does.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Bent light.</strong> Press <strong>Lensing</strong> above the picture, then <strong>An Einstein ring</strong>. Write the <strong>Einstein ring radius</strong>, the <strong>Mass inside the ring (lensing)</strong> and the <strong>Stars and gas inside it</strong> in Analysis {#}. Press <strong>Lensing by the stars and gas alone</strong> and look at the ring.",
"<strong>Part {#}. The Bullet Cluster.</strong> Press <strong>The Bullet Cluster</strong> above the picture, then <strong>The Bullet Cluster today</strong>. Read <strong>The mass map peaks on</strong>, and look at where the green lines circle compared with the pink gas and the yellow galaxies: how does that compare with your prediction in Before the lab {#}? Write the <strong>Distance between the clusters’ galaxies</strong> and the <strong>Small cluster’s gas left behind its galaxies</strong> in Analysis {#}. Then press <strong>The Bullet Cluster with no dark matter</strong> and read where its map peaks.",
